<h2 id="measure">Can Apple Watch measure cortisol?</h2>
<p><strong>No. Apple Watch does not measure cortisol directly.</strong> It records signals like heart rate, heart rate variability (HRV) and sleep. Apps can use these to describe stress and recovery patterns.</p>
<p>A wellness score from a wearable is not the same as a lab cortisol result.</p>
<p>This matters if you searched for "Apple Watch cortisol" or a cortisol tracker app. Cortisol+ helps you explore your wearable trends. It can't tell you how much cortisol is in your blood, saliva or urine.</p>
<p>Want to see those signals as a daily stress score? Cortisol+ is free to download for iPhone and Apple Watch (analysis needs a subscription or eligible trial). Or <a href="/compare/apple-watch-stress-apps/">compare five Apple Watch stress apps</a> first.</p>

<h2 id="track">Does Apple Watch track cortisol?</h2>
<p><strong>No.</strong> Apple Watch does not track cortisol, and Apple Health has no cortisol reading from the Watch.</p>
<p>What it can track over time is heart rate, HRV and sleep. Those trends show how your body is responding, not how much cortisol you have.</p>

<h2 id="signals">What the Watch records, and what it can't tell you</h2>
<div class="signals not-prose">
  <section class="signal" aria-labelledby="sig-hr">
    <h3 id="sig-hr">Heart rate</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>What it records</dt><dd>Beats per minute, from background checks and workouts</dd>
      <dt>Good for</dt><dd>Seeing your activity and resting patterns</dd>
      <dt>Limit</dt><dd>A faster pulse has many causes</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
  <section class="signal" aria-labelledby="sig-hrv">
    <h3 id="sig-hrv">HRV</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>What it records</dt><dd>SDNN, a measure of how much the gaps between beats vary</dd>
      <dt>Good for</dt><dd>Comparing readings with your own history</dd>
      <dt>Limit</dt><dd>Not a cortisol level or a diagnosis</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
  <section class="signal" aria-labelledby="sig-sleep">
    <h3 id="sig-sleep">Sleep</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>What it records</dt><dd>Estimated time asleep, and sleep stages on supported models</dd>
      <dt>Good for</dt><dd>Looking at sleep next to how your days feel</dd>
      <dt>Limit</dt><dd>Can't tell you why you slept badly</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
  <section class="signal" aria-labelledby="sig-temp">
    <h3 id="sig-temp">Wrist temperature</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>What it records</dt><dd>Overnight changes, on supported models</dd>
      <dt>Good for</dt><dd>Extra context when things change from your normal</dd>
      <dt>Limit</dt><dd>Depends on model and settings; not a cortisol sensor</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
</div>
<p>Apple explains <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/120277">heart rate and HRV measurement</a>, <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/108906">sleep tracking</a> and <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/102674">nightly wrist temperature</a>. Follow the steps for your own Watch and software version.</p>

<ZenPicture src="/app-ui/start/zen_watch.webp" caption="Your Watch records heart, HRV and sleep. Not cortisol." />

<h2 id="check-readings">How do I check cortisol levels on Apple Watch?</h2>
<p><strong>You can't check cortisol levels on Apple Watch. There is no cortisol reading to open.</strong></p>
<p>Instead, look at your heart rate, HRV and sleep records, which are the signals stress apps use. Keep those separate from any hormone test result.</p>
<ol>
  <li>On your iPhone, open Health and search for Heart Rate Variability. Look at the samples and their times.</li>
  <li>Look at Resting Heart Rate and Sleep for the same dates. Check which device the data came from.</li>
  <li>If a reading is missing, use the <a href="/blog/apple-watch-hrv-not-updating/">HRV troubleshooting checklist</a> before you trust an app's score.</li>
  <li>For a tidier view, see <a href="/cortisol/track/#cortisol-tracking-app">what a cortisol tracking app does</a> and check the inputs behind it.</li>
</ol>
<p><a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/120277">Apple's heart-rate page</a> explains the light-based (optical) sensor and which features depend on your model. Those readings are not a hormone test. <a href="https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/cortisol-test/">Cortisol testing uses a sample from your body</a>.</p>

<h2 id="cortisol-tracker">Is there a cortisol tracker for Apple Watch?</h2>
<p><strong>Not one that measures the hormone.</strong> Apps sold as a cortisol tracker for Apple Watch, including Cortisol+, read your Watch data from Apple Health and work out a score.</p>
<p>That score is an interpretation of HRV, heart rate and sleep. It is not a cortisol level.</p>
<p>A good cortisol tracker app shows which inputs it used and admits what it can't know. See the <a href="#trust">three questions to ask</a> before you trust one.</p>

<h2>Does Apple Watch have a stress score?</h2>
<p>Apple's Mindfulness features help with breathing, reflection and logging your State of Mind. A mood check-in is your own report. A stress score from another app is an interpretation of sensor data.</p>
<p>They describe different things and may not agree.</p>
<p>Cortisol+ combines the signals it can get into its own wellness score. Other apps use different inputs and formulas. So <strong>a 60 in one app doesn't mean the same as a 60 in another</strong>.</p>

<h2 id="start">How do I start tracking stress with Apple Watch?</h2>
<p><strong>Start with the data your Watch already records.</strong> Check your HRV and heart-rate history in Apple Health. Then make sure your sleep is being recorded.</p>
<p>Add an app when you want those signals turned into trends or a summary. In Cortisol+, a good next step is to open the score's input breakdown and compare it with how you felt that day.</p>
<p>Cortisol+ for iPhone lets you explore the Apple Health data you choose to share. Analysis needs a subscription or eligible trial. Comparing options? Our <a href="/compare/apple-watch-stress-apps/">five-app guide</a> covers Cortisol+, Welltory, StressWatch, Bevel and Athlytic.</p>

<ZenTip image="/app-ui/zen_new_tour_now.webp">
  Before you look at any score, check <strong>when</strong> your last HRV reading was taken. An old reading can make today's number look strange.
</ZenTip>

