<h2>Why can readings be missing or change unexpectedly?</h2>
<p>If your latest sample is missing, follow our <a href="/blog/apple-watch-hrv-not-updating/">Apple Watch HRV troubleshooting guide</a>. It shows you how to compare Apple Health times, check app permissions and review Watch settings.</p>
<p>How often each signal is measured depends on the signal, your Watch model and your settings. Data must also sync to Apple Health before an app can use it.</p>
<p>Several things can affect recording:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The Watch itself:</strong> movement, fit, charging, permissions and software settings.</li>
  <li><strong>Your body and day:</strong> exercise, illness, alcohol, medication, breathing and time of day can all change HRV and heart rate.</li>
</ul>
<p>These are reasons to check what was going on. Don't assume every change means mental stress.</p>

<h2 id="testing">Wearable stress versus cortisol testing</h2>
<p><a href="https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/cortisol-test/">Clinical cortisol tests use blood, saliva or urine</a>. A clinician picks the timing and reads the result based on the question they're looking into.</p>
<p><strong>HRV can't confirm or rule out a hormone disorder.</strong> If you have ongoing or worrying symptoms, see a doctor. Don't use a Watch score to decide whether you need a hormone test.</p>
<p>For a practical next step, try our <a href="/resources/stress-sleep-journal/">free observation journal</a>. To learn about the signal itself, read <a href="/hrv/apple-watch/">HRV on Apple Watch</a> and <a href="/hrv/normal-range/">HRV ranges and measurement methods</a>.</p>

<h2>Explore the questions on Perplexity</h2>
<p>We asked Perplexity about <a href="/resources/perplexity/#apple-watch-apps">Apple Watch cortisol-tracking apps</a> and <a href="/resources/perplexity/#hrv-sleep-stress">using HRV and sleep to understand stress patterns</a>. You can read the shared conversations, the exact prompts and our source notes.</p>
<p>These are AI answers the Cortisol+ team asked for. They are not independent product reviews.</p>

<ZenTip image="/app-ui/zen_new_low.webp">
  Judge an app by how well it <strong>explains</strong> your data, not by how nice its number looks.
</ZenTip>

<h2 id="trust">Three questions before trusting a cortisol tracker app</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Is this a sensor reading or a calculated score?</strong> Look for named inputs and units. A 0–100 wellness score is not a hormone level.</li>
  <li><strong>Can you see the data underneath?</strong> Check the times and any missing inputs before you read the summary. Start with our <a href="/blog/hrv-during-sleep/">overnight HRV checklist</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Does it admit what it doesn't know?</strong> A good app explains its assumptions. It doesn't turn a change in HRV into a diagnosis. <a href="/blog/alcohol-hrv-sleep/">Alcohol and overnight HRV</a> is one example where context matters.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Can an app calculate cortisol from HRV?</h2>
<p><strong>An app can work out an estimate or wellness score from HRV.</strong> But showing a number doesn't prove it measures your cortisol accurately.</p>
<p>Cortisol+ has not shown clinical accuracy against cortisol testing. Read the <a href="/how-it-works/">methodology</a> before you trust its results.</p>

<h2>Which Apple Watch stress app should I choose?</h2>
<p>Compare how each app explains its data, what permissions it asks for and what the subscription offers, not just the color of the score.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/blog/welltory-vs-cortisol-plus/">Welltory versus Cortisol+ comparison</a> explains two approaches. It also says clearly that we publish it. For a wider view, see the <a href="/compare/apple-watch-stress-apps/">Apple Watch stress-app comparison</a>.</p>
